Q: How to remotely kill a process on amazon EC2 linux instance A: <p>One dirty/functional way would be to kill your process via SSH using the java <code>Runtime</code> to execute it. </p>

<p>Something like</p>

<pre><code>Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();
Process p = runtime.exec("ssh user@host command");
</code></pre>

<p>So in your case, if you know the program's PID (1234 for example):</p>

<pre><code>Process p = runtime.exec("ssh user@host kill 1234");
</code></pre>

<p>Or if you know the program's name:</p>

<pre><code>Process p = runtime.exec("ssh user@host pkill my_program_name_x64");
</code></pre>

<p>Note that you usually have to give absolute paths to the executables invoked via runtime. </p>

<p>So you'll have to replace ssh by something like <code>/bin/ssh</code> or <code>/usr/bin/ssh</code> as well as for <code>kill</code> and <code>pkill</code> or <code>killall</code>.</p>
